326642,C4_Full_Context,P1_ZeroShot,1,1,"The PR introduces a significant performance improvement by batching action list width measurements, reducing layout and style recalculations from thousands to just two and twenty-one respectively. The performance gain is substantial, decreasing the duration from 110.2 ms to 7.5 ms. This optimization addresses a known issue (#326640) and includes comprehensive validation through type checking, testing, and layer checks. The PR also adds regression coverage for measurement ordering, toolbar accounting, and width restoration, ensuring that the changes do not introduce new issues.",MERGE,5.05
326642,C4_Full_Context,P2_FewShot,1,1,"The PR introduces a performance improvement by batching action list width measurements, which reduces the number of layout and style recalculations significantly. The performance gain is substantial, reducing the duration from 110.2 ms to 7.5 ms. Additionally, the PR includes regression coverage for measurement ordering, toolbar accounting, and width restoration, ensuring that the changes do not introduce new issues. The code change also includes a defensive check against None values, which is a good practice. The PR is well-documented with a summary of changes, performance metrics, and validation steps.",MERGE,5.38
326642,C4_Full_Context,P3_ChainOfThought,1,1,"The PR introduces a batch action list width measurements feature, which aims to improve performance by reducing the number of layout and style recalculations during row width writes, geometry reads, and restores. The changes are implemented in three phases: alternating write/read/write, batched write/read/write, and toolbar accounting. The implementation is validated through various tests and checks.",MERGE,3.71
326642,C4_Full_Context,P4_RoleBased,1,1,"The PR introduces significant performance improvements by batching action list width measurements, reducing layout recalculations from 9,801 to 2 and style recalculations from 9,801 to 201. The performance gain is substantial, decreasing the duration from 110.2 ms to 7.5 ms. This optimization is crucial for maintaining a smooth user experience, especially with large lists of actions. Additionally, the PR includes regression coverage and validation steps, ensuring that the changes do not introduce new issues. The code diff shows clear improvements in how width measurements are handled, making it easier to understand and maintain.",MERGE,5.93
